'The previous implementation failed because the `ultralytics` training process ignored all specified hyperparameters, falling back to a weak default configuration (`yolov8n-seg`, 2 epochs). This was likely caused by a subtle bug in the argument parser of the library, triggered by the large number of specific data augmentation parameters being passed to the `model.train()` method. My solution is to simplify this call by removing the fine-grained augmentation controls (`fliplr`, `degrees`, `mixup`, etc.) and instead relying on the single, robust `augment=True` flag to enable a default augmentation pipeline. This avoids the parser bug and ensures that the intended large model (`yolov8l-seg`), increased epoch count (200), large image size (1024px), and appropriate batch size are correctly applied, leading to a properly trained and effective model.'
