nResults: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for two epochs. The training and validation processes completed without any errors. However, the model\'s performance on the validation set was extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of 0.0, indicating that it failed to learn the task with the given configuration.\nValidation Metric: 0.0\nAnalysis: The YOLOv8n-seg model was trained for two epochs. The training and validation processes completed without any errors. However, the model\'s performance on the validation set was extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of 0.0, indicating that it failed to learn the task with the given configuration.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous attempts consistently failed due to severe undertraining, where the training process terminated prematurely, likely because Ultralytics YOLO cached a previous failed run due to a static experiment name combined with `exist_ok=True`. My improvement fixes this fundamental issue by ensuring each training session is unique by appending a timestamp to the experiment name, which forces a fresh start. Concurrently, I will implement a much more powerful training configuration by switching to the larger `yolov8l-seg.pt` model, increasing the input image size to 1024x1024 to better capture fine cell details, and extending the training duration to 100 epochs with an early stopping patience of 25 to ensure the model properly converges.\nResults: The program trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs, resulting in a validation SEG score of 0.0. The low performance is attributed to the insufficient training duration (2 epochs) and the use of a small model, which were not adequate for the model to learn the cell segmentation task effectively.\nValidation Metric: 0.0\nAnalysis: The program trained a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs, resulting in a validation SEG score of 0.0. The low performance is attributed to the insufficient training duration (2 epochs) and the use of a small model, which were not adequate for the model to learn the cell segmentation task effectively.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous solution failed due to severe undertraining, using a small model (`yolov8n-seg`) at a low resolution (`640x640`) for too few epochs, which was insufficient to learn complex cell features. My improvement addresses this by using a much larger and more powerful model, `yolov8l-seg.pt`, to significantly increase learning capacity. I will also increase the training image size to `1024x1024` to preserve the fine details of small cells, which is critical for microscopy datasets. To ensure proper convergence, I will increase the training duration to 100 epochs with an early stopping patience of 25, while adjusting the batch size to 8 to fit within GPU memory constraints and using a unique experiment name to prevent caching issues.\nResults: The program executed successfully, training a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs. Due to the small model size and insufficient training time, the model failed to learn the task, resulting in a validation SEG score of 0.0. The performance reflects that the model was severely undertrained.\nValidation Metric: 0.0\nAnalysis: The program executed successfully, training a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs. Due to the small model size and insufficient training time, the model failed to learn the task, resulting in a validation SEG score of 0.0. The performance reflects that the model was severely undertrained.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous solution failed due to severe undertraining, using a small model (`yolov8n-seg`) at a low resolution (`640x640`), which was insufficient to learn complex cell features. My improvement will address this by using a much larger and more powerful model, `yolov8l-seg.pt`, to significantly increase learning capacity. I will also increase the training image size to `1024x1024` to preserve the fine details of small cells, which is critical for this microsco