ful model, `yolov8l-seg.pt`, which has a greater capacity to learn complex cell features. I will increase the training duration to 100 epochs with an early stopping patience of 25 to ensure the model converges properly. Furthermore, I will increase the input image size to 1024x1024, which is crucial for preserving the fine details of small cells in microscopy images, and adjust the batch size to 8 to manage GPU memory usage with the larger model and images.\nResults: The script successfully ran a baseline training process using a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs. The training and evaluation completed without any execution errors. However, the model\'s performance was extremely poor, resulting in a final SEG score of 0.0. The validation metrics (mAP, Precision, Recall) were all near-zero, indicating the model failed to learn the segmentation task effectively, which is expected given the very short training duration.\nValidation Metric: 0.0\nAnalysis: The script successfully ran a baseline training process using a YOLOv8n-seg model for 2 epochs. The training and evaluation completed without any execution errors. However, the model\'s performance was extremely poor, resulting in a final SEG score of 0.0. The validation metrics (mAP, Precision, Recall) were all near-zero, indicating the model failed to learn the segmentation task effectively, which is expected given the very short training duration.\n\n-------------------------------\nDesign: The previous solution failed completely, likely because the `yolov8n-seg` model was too small and the `640x640` image resolution was too low to capture the fine details of the cells. My improvement addresses this by using a significantly more powerful model, `yolov8l-seg.pt`, which has a much greater capacity for learning complex features. To ensure crucial details are not lost during training, I will also increase the input image size to `1024x1024`. To accommodate the larger model and image size on the GPU, the batch size will be r