ning configuration to use the powerful `yolov8l-seg.pt` model, which has the necessary capacity for this complex task. We will train at a high resolution (`imgsz=1024`) to preserve the fine details of the cells and for a sufficient number of epochs (100) with early stopping to ensure convergence. To prevent potential caching issues that may have caused the previous failure, a unique, timestamped experiment name will be used to guarantee a fresh training session.\nResults: The model was trained for only two epochs, which proved to be insufficient for learning the cell segmentation task. The resulting performance on the validation set was extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of 0.0. The validation metrics from the training process, including precision, recall, and mAP, were all near zero, confirming the model did not learn to generate accurate segmentations.\nValidation Metric: 0.0\nAnalysis: The model was trained for only two epochs, which proved to be insufficient for learning the cell segmentation task. The resulting performance on the validation set was extremely poor, achieving a final SEG score of 0.0. The validation metrics from the training process, including precision, recall, and mAP, were all near zero, confirming the model did not learn to generate accurate segmentations.\n\n-------------------------------\nDebug plan: The previous attempt failed due to severe undertraining, using a small `yolov8n-seg` model at a low resolution for only two epochs, which was insufficient for this complex task. My solution addresses this by using a significantly larger and more capable model, `yolov8l-seg`, to better capture the intricate features of the cells. I will increase the training image size to 1024x1024 to preserve fine details crucial for segmenting small objects in microscopy images. To ensure the model converges properly, I will extend the training duration to 100 epochs with an early stopping patience of 25, while adjusting the batch size to 8 to manage